WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
If you can digitize sound and pictures on a Macintosh.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.000
There's no telling what you can do with a computer.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
This is Hyper Animator, yet one more fascinating hyper card application,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
here at Boston's Macworld Expo.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Today we take you on a guided tour of the biggest ever Macworld Expo,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
on this edition of the Computer Chronicles.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:31.000
♪♪

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:42.000
♪♪

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.000
The Computer Chronicles is made possible in part by McGraw-Hill,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.000
publishers of Byte Magazine, and Bix, the Byte Information Exchange.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
In print and online, Byte and Bix serve computer professionals worldwide

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.000
with detailed information on new hardware, software, and technologies.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Welcome to the Computer Chronicles.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.000
I'm Stuart Shafae, and with me this week at the Macworld Expo here in Boston

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.000
is the editor-in-chief of Macworld Magazine, Jerry Burrell.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Jerry, the Americans and the Russians are making progress toward peace.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Warring factions all over the world seem to be settling their differences.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
It seems the major war left is between PC users and Mac users.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.000
How's that war going?

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.000
One has the impression that the Macworld, if I can borrow that phrase,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
has been gaining a lot of ground over the past year.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Well, it's all over the show floor.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.000
We've got hundreds of products, developers from the PC side,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.000
well-known names of all.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.000
The Mac 2 paid off.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Apple gambled properly, and they've allowed developers to come in

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.000
and to expand their market for them.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The opening up of the Mac with the Mac 2 in particular.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Exactly. It's been very successful, and we see it here.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.000
The second thing is that while we're making the transition on the PC side

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.000
from Windows over into OS 2, PS 2, there are simply no products there,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
and they're coming very slowly.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
So these new Mac 2 products are taking advantage of the fact

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
that the IBM market is in somewhat of a disarray.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Jerry, today we'll take a look at all those new Macintosh products

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.000
you were talking about as we take you on a guided tour

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
of this biggest ever Macworld Expo.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Now, as usual at a Macintosh show, one of the big subjects is graphics,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
so we'll start out by taking a look at a whole new generation

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
of graphics software for the Macintosh.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
If there were any lingering doubt about the appeal of splashy graphics,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.000
it was not apparent at this year's Boston Macworld Expo.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
In almost every aisle of the World Trade Center,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000
paint, video, and architectural CAD programs were highly promoted and prominent.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Among these, there were a few brand-new packages and a lot of 2.0s.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.000
ABBA software introduced Graphist Paint 2,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.000
a paint package with a hidden stencil layer beneath the work layer.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.000
An artist can etch away sections of the top image

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.000
to reveal portions of the underlying image.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Finished images can be rotated, skewed, and distorted or inscribed on a sphere.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Graphist Paint 2 needs at least 2 megabytes of RAM and retails for $495.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Cricut software unveiled Cricut Paint,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.000
a monochrome program with a feature called Fresh Paint.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.000
A bit-mapped drawing can become an object for moving and manipulating.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Deselecting the object turns it back into a bit-mapped image.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Drawing tools can be customized to suit the user's preferences.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Cricut Paint sells for $295.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Well, while everybody else is focusing on the color painting world,

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.000
what they've forgotten is there's about a million and a half black-and-white paint users,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.000
and a lot of the materials that are produced in desktop publishing packages

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
are still being produced on black-and-white devices, such as Postgres.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Deneba software brought out its latest 2.0 version of Canvas,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.000
a color paint package offering an unlimited number of layers.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Images can be viewed either as objects or bitmaps in up to 16 million colors.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Canvas 2.0 also offers gray levels in 1% increments

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.000
and drawing precision up to 1 64,000th of an inch.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.000
The release price is $299.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Micro Illusions offered what it called third-generation graphics

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.000
in a new paint program called Photon Paint.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
With Photon Paint, an artist can tilt, rotate, resize, and map an image onto a 3-D object.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
You can also blend colors together smoothly through a process called dithering

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.000
onto either transparent or opaque backgrounds.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Photon Paint retails for about $300.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.000
At the Silicon Beach booth, Super Paint 2.0 was on display,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.000
a budget-priced black-and-white image processor

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.000
offering new poster, edge, and auto-trace functions.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
We've added some strong features to the paint part of the program,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
in particular the plug-in modules where somebody can make a custom tool

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
and add it to a slot in the program and it appears in the tool palette.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.000
The airbrush is really terrific on the paint side.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.000
On the draw side, we've got Bezier curves, the auto-trace,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.000
where it will automatically trace a bitmap image, put it in the draw layer

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.000
with object-oriented, including Bezier curves.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.000
And pre-rotation of objects and text, those are the main things.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.000
Silicon Beach also officially released Digital Darkroom,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.000
a program with a set of so-called intelligent tools

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.000
for automating most cut-and-paste, select, and fill operations.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Super Paint sells for $199, while Digital Darkroom retails for $395.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.000
AbVent took the wraps off Jonathan Draw,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.000
a two-dimensional drawing program that reads and prints

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.000
to the best resolution of your output device,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.000
independently of the screen resolution you are using.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Jonathan Draw also features SnapTools for jumping between different views.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.000
The program will sell for $495.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
Apple's software spin-off company, Claris, unveiled the two-dimensional Clariscad,

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.000
offering mouse or keyboard entry, automatic dimensioning,

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
and an adjustable workspace.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.000
The user can adapt or design new fill patterns, pens, dashed lines,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.000
and dimensioning parameters.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:29.000
Clariscad is expected to ship in December and will retail for $799.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.000
For architectural designers concerned with the insides of a building,

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.000
Kump Servco demonstrated Mac Interiors,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
a $295 3D interior design tool.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.000
With Mac Interiors, a user can create a room outline,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.000
including doors and windows, create a library of objects

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.000
with up to 64 sides each, and then place the objects in the room.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.000
The room and its contents can be viewed from any angle

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.000
to any user-defined scale.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Mac Interiors can send output to a laser printer, image writer,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.000
or large-scale plotter.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.000
In the high-end CAD category, Dynaware premiered its Mac 2 version

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.000
of Dyna Perspective, a 3D CAD animation package

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.000
featuring up to 120 colors, floating point coordinates,

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:20.000
and five different grid units.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.000
To create an animated walkthrough of your drawing,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.000
just select a few views, specify the number of frames in between,

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:31.000
and Dyna Perspective will generate the intervening frames.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.000
The program requires a minimum of 2 megabytes of RAM

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:39.000
and sells for $1,495.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.000
Mac Architryon, a 2D and 3D package for architects from Jimeor,

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.000
made its debut at the show, promising an interface

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.000
that mimics traditional architectural tools.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.000
Jimeor is marketing the program to professional users

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:59.000
who need to design in real volumetric 3D from start to finish.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:03.000
A designer can visualize and animate a project in 3D

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.000
and create two-dimensional drawings from the same data.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.000
Mac Architryon is priced at around $1,500.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:16.000
To physically help visualize the increasingly complex graphics on the Mac 2,

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.000
eMachines unveiled a new display called the Big Picture Z21.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.000
The 21-inch monochrome monitor can display at four different

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.000
viewing resolutions from 30 to 80 dots per inch.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.000
Small format publications can be viewed full size on the screen.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.000
Larger formats, the Z21, offers hardware level panning and zooming.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.000
Maximum resolution is 1280 by 960,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.000
and a grayscale model will display 256 levels of gray.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.000
Prices start at around $2,500.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.000
For users not satisfied with drawing from scratch,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.000
Pixelogic brought out the ProViz Video Digitizer,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.000
a standalone peripheral that connects through the Mac's SCSI port.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.000
The ProViz digitizes images from any video source

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:09.000
and will send the digitized output to a monitor, printer, or a videotape recorder.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.000
The digitizer is available in a monochrome version for $10.95

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.000
and a color version for $16.95.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.000
The Macintosh has always been known for its good graphics,

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.000
its friendly user interface, things like desktop publishing,

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.000
but the real growth for the Mac in the last year

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:29.000
has been the explosion of mainstream business applications.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.000
Just as desktop publishing was the software star a few years ago,

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:38.000
desktop presentation software seemed to be the must-have business application this year,

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.000
with a number of familiar names throwing their hats into the ring.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.000
Aldis, better known for originating desktop publishing,

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.000
demonstrated a pre-release version of Persuasion,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:56.000
a presentation package that combines an idea outliner with pre-designed visual templates.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.000
The user enters text into the outliner,

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.000
picks one of the auto-templates,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:06.000
and the program formats the text into a visual presentation.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.000
Persuasion comes with drawing and charting tools,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.000
and the templates can be customized to contain corporate logos and colors.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:18.000
Persuasion will be available in early 1989 for around $500.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.000
Basically, the differences are this product has a full set of tools in one package,

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:26.000
where most of the other products are missing a major component,

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.000
such as charting and graphing or outline processing.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:33.000
It's a complete set of tools, and the major difference is the auto-template technology,

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:38.000
which is the underlying capability that automatically lays out slides from outline text.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:43.000
Lectraset stood out with its version of presentation software called Standout,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.000
an integrated package including word processor,

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:52.000
an online design and creation system, and integrated charts and graphs.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.000
Standout comes with a library of templates, or a user can create and save custom designs.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.000
The program offers three levels of viewing magnification

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.000
and a group of master frames for placing repeated elements.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.000
The price stands at $295.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.000
Microsoft used the show to introduce several new Mac 2 products.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.000
PowerPoint 2.0 for desktop presentations in living color,

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:21.000
Works 2.0, and Excel version 1.5.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:26.000
The update to Excel features new macro functions, custom menus and dialog boxes,

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.000
and of course color support for the Mac 2.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:35.000
For users who can't wait, Excel supports Apple's Multifinder multitasking operating system.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:44.000
Microsoft Works sells for $295, while PowerPoint 2.0 and Excel 1.5 both retail for $395.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:50.000
Ashton Tate announced shipment of a presentation-oriented spreadsheet called Full Impact.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.000
The package has an integrated text processor, pull-down menus,

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.000
and allows up to eight spreadsheets to be opened at once.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.000
Full Impact also features a worksheet to assemble spreadsheet data,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:05.000
text and charts into a final presentation package.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:09.000
The suggested retail price is around $400.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.000
What we've done is taken the core spreadsheet functionality,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.000
all the features you'd expect to find in a sophisticated spreadsheet,

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:19.000
and added around that presentation capabilities that you'd associate with desktop publishing,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:23.000
including some drawing capabilities, text processing capabilities,

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:27.000
and graphics which are incorporated within the spreadsheet and not separate.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:33.000
Desktop publishing continues to attract an audience at computer shows, and Macworld Expo is no exception.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.000
Springboard Software unveiled Springboard Publisher,

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:42.000
a $200 package that fits in somewhere between Desktop Publisher and Word Processor.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:46.000
Springboard Publisher integrates page layout, word processor, and graphics

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:50.000
with optional newsletter templates, clip art, and laser fonts.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:54.000
Expected release date for the Mac 2 version is December.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:59.000
In the database category, ProView Development introduced Panorama,

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.000
a database with a spreadsheet-like structure.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.000
Drawing tools allow the user to create and modify forms and reports,

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.000
and to import graphics from other applications.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:14.000
A feature called Flash Art allows the user to paste artwork into database records

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:18.000
simply by typing the name of a pre-stored image.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:30.000
♪♪

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.000
For years, Macintosh users have had to put up with a lot of snide remarks from PC users

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.000
when the subject turned to color.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:38.000
Well, not anymore.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:44.000
With the advent of the Mac 2, there are all kinds of software applications here at Macworld Expo

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.000
that take advantage of the new color capability of the Macintosh.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.000
Like the laser printer that paved the way to desktop publishing,

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.000
the Color Macintosh has attracted a vast range of color software and hardware.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.000
EGES development showed the first module of its future animation workshop

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:08.000
called Showcase FX for color title generation and special effects.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.000
The program can embellish text with three-dimensional effects,

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:18.000
adding border outlines, neon, embossing, an offset edge, and horizontal or vertical spread.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:22.000
Showcase features a WYSIWYG, or what you see is what you get, display,

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:29.000
and metamorphic fonts that can be stretched, dragged, and skewed to create unique shapes.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:33.000
The user can animate titles and playback linked sequences.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:40.000
Showcase FX sells for $395 and needs 2 megabytes of RAM.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:47.000
Macromind unveiled VideoWorks Professional, a $695 real-time color animation program.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:52.000
The new version of VideoWorks comes with animation tools to create airbrush speckles,

00:14:52.000 --> 00:15:00.000
gradients, cycling, smearing, smudging, and tinting, and can pick up, rotate, and slide images.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.000
Drawings can also be auto-animated.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:09.000
At some booths, it was difficult to tell if one was looking at a computer monitor or a television screen.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:17.000
The merging of computer graphics with video images made the video capture board a common, if expensive, peripheral.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:21.000
TruVizion demonstrated its video capture board called NuVista,

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:27.000
which can display video images in high spatial and high resolution color.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:33.000
NuVista offers multiple-bit depths, programmable capture, and comes with a 32-bit coprocessor.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:40.000
Prices start at $4,250 with 2 megabytes of onboard memory.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:49.000
Mass Microsystems brought out the ColorSpace2 video board, featuring graphics overlay and auto genlock to video signals.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:54.000
The board can output RGB and American Standard NTSC video simultaneously,

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:59.000
and is also compatible with European PAL television.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:03.000
Among new paint programs, Electronic Arts premiered Studio 8,

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:11.000
a $495 color paint package with 4 levels of magnification and 9 tool modifiers.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:19.000
Studio 8 has a special tool to allow any portion of the image to be picked up and used as a paintbrush to create custom effects.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:28.000
A magnified window can be opened anywhere on the screen, and all tools are usable at both regular and magnified levels.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:37.000
SuperMac presented Pixel Paint Professional, a $595 program featuring Pantone colors for pre-press preparation.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:42.000
Users can scan through colors interactively as if thumbing through a book of samples.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:52.000
Pixel Paint Professional comes with a full set of masking tools, a built-in color separator, and spot color separation keyed to Pantone colors.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:58.000
I think in 84, people were going ahead and saying the Mac is wonderful because it's got icons and windows.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:05.000
We're beginning to see those on other machines, and the strength that we're seeing now is the ability to support true color and advanced imaging.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000
I think that's really going to be the thing that sets it apart.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:16.000
To help non-artists save time with their paint programs, Image Club unveiled a new clip art library on CD-ROM called Art Room.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:30.000
For $1,000, you get 70 megabytes of ready-to-print images, including 1,000 encapsulated PostScript images, 100 typefaces, publishing templates, a font juggler, and an image retriever.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.000
Kodak and Photomac were jointly demonstrating a color slide scanning, processing, and printing system.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:47.000
The Kodak desktop scanner takes about three minutes to scan a color transparency at 2,800 dots per inch and 12 bits per pixel color.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:56.000
Photomac software retouches and color corrects images, which can then be printed on a Kodak Color Thermal Printer.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:05.000
The Iconics scanner sells for $8,900. The software is around $700, and the printer costs under $5,000.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:15.000
For those lacking something to scan, Presentation Technologies was offering the Montage FR-1, a high-resolution film recorder.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:23.000
The Montage can produce slides for more than 16 million colors, has full bitmap capabilities, and 4,000 lines of resolution.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:27.000
Montage sells for around $6,000.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:37.000
A scanner of a different color was Apple's contribution to the show, a flatbed optical image scanner complete with AppleScan and HyperScan software.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:48.000
The monochrome Apple scanner can process line art, halftones, and grayscale images at up to 300 dots per inch, grayscale at 4 bits or 16 levels per pixel.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:53.000
The Apple scanner retails for around $1,800.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:19:01.000
Also available at the Apple booth was Senior Vice President Jean-Louis Gasset, who offered some thoughts on the status of the OpenMac.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:07.000
Again, it's a product that the marketplace took over and did things, because we cannot think of everything.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:19.000
And the charm of a flexible, open, modular product is that if it's flexible, and if you can put the pieces together with enough ease of implementation,

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:25.000
and if you have enough power in the combination, then the people who know something that you don't know will take care of it.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.000
That's what they do in the marketplace.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:32.000
For a while, the battle was, does Macintosh have software? Certainly that's not a question anymore.

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:38.000
Now you can just pick the finest of the finest, and that's a much better position to be in.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:51.000
Sharp Electronics introduced a new color scanner, the JX450, a $7,000 desktop machine with 300 dots per inch resolution and 64 shade gradations for each RGB element.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:57.000
A single charged couple device reads the RGB data and converts it into 8-bit digital data.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:03.000
The scanner has a color tone capacity of over 260,000 shades.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:10.000
Almost as rare as color scanners are color printers, but Haltech had one called Pixel Master.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:17.000
Definitely not for the desktop, the towering Pixel Master boasts an advanced thermal ink process called Thermojet.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:26.000
The printer has an unusual vertical printhead that squirts liquefied plastic inks onto the paper, which solidify instantly upon contact.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:31.000
Up to 250,000 different shades are available at 240 dots per inch.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:35.000
Pixel Master is priced at under $6,000.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:44.000
Some striking color graphics and animation were on display at the Invention software booth, produced with a package called Professional Programmer's Extender.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:51.000
The sample application included animated cross-sections of three-dimensional medical data.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:59.000
Despite the product exclusivity of Macworld Expo, connectivity to the rest of the computer world was offered by a number of vendors.

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:06.000
3Com announced Macintosh support for its multi-vendor 3-plus open architecture, LAN Manager.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Well, in our networks, which are generally on the high end of networks, we sell a series of dedicated network servers.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:20.000
And in the last six months, we've observed that now more than a third of those servers, which you can connect to IBM PCs and other devices,

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:23.000
more than a third are going out with Macintosh capabilities.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:27.000
So we now make the Mac software a standard and just put it in all our servers.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:38.000
One of the most unusual output devices of the show had to be the Poem, a personal and original embroidery machine from Enzen Hoshigumi Company.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:43.000
Poem is the first of a new group of products for what the company calls desktop embroidery.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:52.000
From an original sketch or a library of clip art, Poem will embroider a matching design on its tiny sewing machine, one color at a time.

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:56.000
The complete kit sells for under $1,000.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:03.000
Finally, BrightStar technology showed yet another offbeat application of Apple's HyperCard called HyperAnimator.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:13.000
BrightStar's animation program records faces and lip movements and recreates them phonetically on screen to create the impression of synchronized speech.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:19.000
It will convert typed text into a phonetic stream of vaguely human-sounding speech.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:23.000
HyperCard phonetics can get a copy for $150.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:30.000
Jerry, as you said at the very beginning of this program, there really are a lot of new Macintosh products here at Macworld Expo.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.000
How would you sort of sum up the show for us? What were the highlights here for you?

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:40.000
Well, by subject area, desktop publishing, color for the Macintosh, graphics, computer-aided design,

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:44.000
and an extension of vertical applications into the business marketplace.

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:50.000
Not just the word processors and spreadsheets and databases, but things like accounting, a lot of new products.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:54.000
Okay, let me put you on the line now. Specifically, what were the really hot products at this show?

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:59.000
Well, my pick of the show probably should start with Excel 1.5 for color.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:02.000
Color spreadsheets make life a lot more pleasant.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:10.000
We've got Iconics and Kodak that have a slide capture, 35mm digitization package and software for display.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:17.000
Dynoware, an interesting new company from Japan that are showing an architectural CAD, color rendering of computer-aided design.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:23.000
Aldus' Persuasion had to be the hottest business presentation package on the show floor out here.

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:30.000
Studio 8, an electronics arts package, which is a color paint package for the Macintosh. A lot of fun for that.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:37.000
The TruVizion cart, 32-bit color, true color for the Macintosh and video. We hope it's NTSC.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:43.000
Montage, for the first time now we can produce 35mm slides. They're showing it on the floor and it's shipping.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:49.000
And finally, Avalon prepress software for the publishing industry that allow us to touch up with color.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:54.000
Jerry, thanks for being here. That's our special show on the Macworld Expo here in Boston.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:58.000
Hope we'll see you again here next week on the Computer Chronicles.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:09.000
♪♪♪

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:15.000
In the random access file this week, the NeXT Computer finally made its debut in San Francisco last week,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:23.000
introduced by NeXT president Steve Jobs. Jobs said the NeXT Computer has mainframe power and will sell for $6,500,

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:29.000
far less than half the price of similar current generation products. Jobs said the machine will be shipped to selected universities

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:34.000
before the end of this year and will generally be available in the second quarter of next year.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:40.000
The NeXT Computer features a high-resolution 17-inch megapixel display in black and white.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:45.000
The computer itself is a one-foot black magnesium cube with 8 megabytes of main memory.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:49.000
The NeXT Computer has built-in transparent networking and multitasking.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:53.000
The machine comes bundled with a new Unix user interface and other software.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:25:01.000
The whole computer is on one board with two main processing chips, including the Motorola 68030 running at 25 MHz.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:09.000
And as rumored, it features CD-quality sound from a 10 MIPS sound chip and a 256-megabyte internal optical disk drive

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:16.000
with full read, write and erase capabilities. NeXT also unveiled a laser printer with 400 dots per inch resolution

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:19.000
and a price tag under $2,000.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:24.000
Digital Equipment Corporation is returning to the PC marketplace with a deal to sell Tandy PCs.

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:31.000
DEC will also service Tandy computers owned by DEC customers, and there was a technology trade agreement between the two companies,

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:36.000
which means Tandy will be able to access DEC's networking technology called DECnet.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:41.000
Digital says it won't sell the Tandy 5000, which uses microchannel architecture.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:44.000
The Japanese showed off a whole string of new laptops last week.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:50.000
Toshiba beefed up the high end of its portable line with a new 386 portable called the T5200.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:57.000
It features a VGA-compatible gas plasma display and a 40-megabyte hard drive. It runs only on AC.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:02.000
But Toshiba did introduce a new 12-megahertz 286 laptop that does run on batteries.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:08.000
It comes with a 20-megabyte hard drive, weighs 11 pounds and is priced under $5,000.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:11.000
NEC was also rolling out laptops this week.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:17.000
The smallest was a four-pounder called the Ultralight that features 640K and internal 2400-watt modem,

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:21.000
a backlit display and a 2-megabyte RAM drive.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:25.000
And Sharp has introduced a new 8-ounce handheld computer called the Wizard.

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:30.000
It interfaces with a PC and features the usual personal organizer functions.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.000
Ziff Communications is now shipping its new CD-ROM-based computer reference library.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:40.000
It features full-text articles from the last year's worth of 10 major computer publications,

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:46.000
plus abstracts of computer-related articles from 120 other technology periodicals.

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:52.000
Finally, the East Coast beat the West Coast in the first-ever Computer Bowl held in Boston last week.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:57.000
The West Coast team captain, David Bunnell, said it was only because the East Coast team studied.

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:00.000
In the West, he said, we were too busy being successful and making money.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.000
That's it for this week's Chronicles. We'll see you next time.

